  "Nanoplasty is a formaldehyde-free hair treatment that smooths and straightens with nano-sized molecules. How it works, cost, safety, and how it compares.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Is nanoplasty better than keratin?",
    a: "Not universally — it often smooths more deeply, lasts a bit longer, and uses lower heat, but it's usually pricier and, like keratin, its \"formaldehyde-free\" status depends on the specific product.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does nanoplasty straighten curly hair?",
    a: "It smooths and significantly relaxes texture, and can straighten wavy to moderately curly hair well. Very tightly coiled hair may not go fully straight from nanoplasty alone.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is nanoplasty formaldehyde-free?",
    a: "It's marketed that way, but claims vary by brand and haven't always held up under testing — verify the ingredient list.",
  },
  {
    q: "How is nanoplasty different from hair botox?",
    a: "Hair botox is a conditioning repair treatment that smooths but doesn't straighten; nanoplasty smooths and straightens and lasts longer.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I do nanoplasty at home?",
    a: "Kits exist, but results depend on sectioning, controlled heat, and correct application. For most people the salon is worth it.",
  },
